Usage: pr [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Convert text files for printing.

Note: This is a simplified implementation. Advanced features
like multi-column layout and complex pagination are not fully
supported.

OPTIONS:
  +PAGE ARG                    begin printing with page PAGE [default 1]
  -COLUMN ARG                  produce COLUMN-column output
  -a                           produce multi-column output
  -d                           double-space the output
  -e, --expand[=ARG]           expand input TABs
  -f, --form-feed              use form feeds instead of newlines
  -h, --header ARG             use a centered HEADER
  -l, --length ARG             set page length
  -n, --number-lines[=ARG]     number lines
  -o, --indent ARG             offset each line
  -r, --no-file-warnings       omit warning when a file cannot be opened
  -s, --separator[=ARG]        separate columns by characters
  -t, --omit-header            omit page headers and trailers
  -T, --omit-pagination        omit page headers and trailers
  -w, --width ARG              set page width
  -W, --page-width ARG         set page width (default 72)
  -b, --balance-columns        balance columns on the last page
  -c, --show-control-chars     use hat notation (^G) and octal backslash
                               notation
  -D, --date-format ARG        use FORMAT for the date in the header
  -F, -f                       use form feeds instead of newlines (same as -f)
  -i, --output-tabs[=ARG]      replace spaces with TABs where possible
  -J, --join-lines             merge full lines (ignore --column warnings)
  -m, --merge                  print all files in parallel, one in each column
  -N, --first-line-number ARG  start counting with NUMBER at line 1 of first
                               page
  -S, --sep-string[=ARG]       separate columns by STRING (default single space)
  -v, --show-nonprinting       use octal backslash notation for non-printing
                               characters
      --across                 print across pages
      --columns ARG            output COLUMN-column output
      --double-space           double-space the output
      --expand-tabs[=ARG]      expand input TABs
      --pages ARG              begin printing with page PAGE
      --help                   display this help and exit
  -V, --version                output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
